prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages student records and grades for an educational institution.

The people involved are instructors, students, parents, and the registrar. One person may hold both the instructor and the parent role at the same time.

The resources are student records, grades, and courses. Every student record records the student, the advisor, the student's age, any FERPA directory-information block, and the status.

Every grade records the student, the course, the person who graded it, and the status.

Every course records its instructor and roster.

Each request carries the current date and whether it comes from the campus network.

An instructor, student, or parent must not view a withdrawn student's record when a FERPA directory-information block is set.

A registrar may view a withdrawn student's record when a FERPA directory-information block is set.

When no FERPA directory-information block is set, this withdrawn-student viewing limit does not apply.

A request by an instructor to view a student record that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity User;

entity Instructor {
    user: User,
};

entity Student {
    user: User,
};

entity Parent {
    user: User,
};

entity Registrar {
    user: User,
};

entity StudentRecord {
    student: Student,
    advisor: Instructor,
    age: Long,
    ferpaDirectoryInformationBlock: Bool,
    status: String,
};

entity Grade {
    student: Student,
    course: Course,
    gradedBy: User,
    status: String,
};

entity Course {
    instructor: Instructor,
    roster: Set<Student>,
};

action viewStudentRecord appliesTo {
    principal: [Instructor, Student, Parent, Registrar],
    resource: [StudentRecord],
    context: {
        currentDate: datetime,
        fromCampusNetwork: Bool,
    },
};
"
}
